acks the molecular-weight term: heating pure hydrogen (M = 2) with fission instead of burning a heavy oxidizer, and since v_e \u221d \u221a(T/M), slashing M from ~18 to 2 overwhelms the lower temperature; and a fission reactor's gigawatt power density keeps thrust high (F = 2P/v_e doesn't bite)", "It uses electric fields like an ion drive", "It runs at higher temperature than chemistry"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Exhaust velocity goes as \u221a(T/M). Chemistry is floored near M \u2248 10\u201318 because it must carry a heavy oxidizer into its own exhaust. Nuclear thermal abandons combustion and heats the lightest possible gas \u2014 pure hydrogen, M = 2 \u2014 with a fission reactor, and the molecular-weight advantage (a factor of ~9 inside the square root) crushes the modest temperature disadvantage. Crucially, a reactor delivers gigawatts, comparable to combustion, so unlike a feeble-powered ion drive it gets high v_e AND high thrust \u2014 the best of both worlds, which is exactly what a crewed Mars mission wants."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "NERVA \u2014 a nuclear thermal engine achieving about 850 s of specific impulse \u2014 was built, tested, and worked, then cancelled in 1973. Why, and what is meant by calling the engine \"a medical device\"?", "options": ["It exploded on the test stand; the phrase is ironic", "It was cancelled for lack of a mission, not any technical failure \u2014 the crewed Mars program it was built for was defunded; it is called a medical device because a nuclear rocket's fast transit cuts the crew's galactic-cosmic-ray dose, so its true product is reduced radiation and cancer risk", "It was banned by an international treaty", "It was too heavy to launch"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "NERVA performed \u2014 doubling chemical specific impulse, demonstrating restart, throttling, and hours of run time, essentially flight-ready. It was cancelled because by 1973 the crewed Mars program had lost political support an